 "pages": [
  {
   "page": 1,
   "body": "Annual Review 2026 1. Service performance Harbourside Council handled more requests in 2026 than in any previous year, and most of them were resolved without a second contact. This review sets out what the council delivered, where it fell short and what residents can expect next year. Figures come from the council's case management system and cover the twelve months to 31 March. Household waste collections ran to schedule on 97 days in every 100. The remaining delays were caused by two weeks of flooding in January, when the main depot on Quay Road was closed and crews worked from a temporary yard. Residents were told by text message on the morning of each affected route, and no collection was missed for more than three days. 1.1 Waste and recycling The recycling rate rose from 41 per cent to 46 per cent after the introduction of weekly food waste collections. Contamination, the share of recycling loads rejected at the sorting plant, fell by a third. The council expects the new rate to hold, although it depends on residents continuing to separate glass and food waste correctly. Garden waste collections remain a paid service. Almost 18,000 households subscribed this year, and the subscription fee will stay at 52 pounds for 2027. Subscribers who move house can transfer the service by telephone or online. 1.2 Parking and transport Residents' parking permits are now issued online in an average of two working days. The infrastructure behind the system was replaced in October, and the old paper process closes at the end of the year. Visitors can still buy daily vouchers from any library or from the council's website.",
   "boilerplate": [
    "Harbourside Council | Annual Review 2026",
    "Page 1 of 4"
   ]
  },
  {
   "page": 2,
   "body": "2. Requests handled The council recorded more than one million three hundred thousand requests across its four busiest services. Waste collection and parking both grew, while library loans fell for the second year running as more residents moved to the digital collection. Table 1: Requests handled by service, in thousands Planning applications are few but slow to handle, which is why they are counted separately from the other services. Every figure in the table is rounded to one decimal place, so the total may differ from the sum of the rows by a tenth.",
   "boilerplate": [
    "Harbourside Council | Annual Review 2026",
    "Page 2 of 4"
   ]
  },
  {
   "page": 3,
   "body": "3. Contact and complaints Most problems are put right by the team that caused them, so the quickest route is to contact that team directly by telephone or through the online form. If you are not satisfied with the reply you can make a formal complaint, and the council will acknowledge it within five working days. 3.1 Making a complaint A complaint should say what went wrong, when it happened and what you would like the council to do about it. Include any reference number you were given, because it lets the case officer find the earlier correspondence at once. The steps are: 1. Write to the service manager, giving your name and a reference number. 2. Wait ten working days for a full written reply from that manager. 3. If you are still unhappy, ask for your complaint to be escalated. 3.2 Escalation An escalated complaint is reviewed by a director who was not involved in the original decision. The director will write to you within twenty working days. If the outcome does not resolve the matter, you may take it to the independent ombudsman, whose service is free.",
   "boilerplate": [
    "Harbourside Council | Annual Review 2026",
    "Page 3 of 4"
   ]
  },
  {
   "page": 4,
   "body": "4. Parking permit application Complete every field in capital letters and attach proof of address. Fields with tick boxes accept one choice, and the council returns incomplete forms unprocessed.",
